# Line endings.
#
# Shell scripts must keep LF endings in the repository. With CRLF, a Linux
# machine reads the shebang as `/bin/bash^M` and refuses to run the file:
#
#     bash: .devcontainer/setup.sh: /bin/bash^M: bad interpreter: No such file
#
# Git Bash on Windows tolerates CRLF, so this only breaks on Linux - which is
# precisely where the one-command setup is meant to run, in a Codespace. Setting
# it here means the file is stored and checked out with LF everywhere.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for anything else that might be executed or parsed by a
# Unix tool.
*.bash text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts, if any are ever added, want the opposite.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
